-- title:
--   Item-language compiler correctness with cost preservation
-- statement:
--   Let `iprog` be an item-language program with $P$ procedures over $S$ item stacks, and let `prog` be a stack-machine program over $S+4$ stacks whose procedures are the compilations of those of `iprog` ($\mathrm{prog}\ j = \mathrm{compile}(\mathrm{iprog}\ j)$ for all $j$). Suppose the item statement $p$ executes (big-step semantics `IExec`, with input word $I$) from item state $s$ to $s'$ with cost $c$. Then
--   1. the compiled statement runs on the encoded states within the same cost:
--   $$\mathrm{Runs}\ I\ \mathrm{prog}\ (\mathrm{compile}\ p)\ (\mathrm{encI}\ s)\ (\mathrm{encI}\ s')\ c;$$
--   2. if moreover $p = \mathrm{loop}\ i\ f\ b$, then $24\le c$ and the inner loop of its compilation runs from $\mathrm{encF}\ i\ f\ s$ (the encoding of $s$ with the loop flag for the test $f$ on stack $i$ pushed on the scratch stack $s_3$) to $\mathrm{encI}\ s'$ with a $0$ flag pushed on $s_3$, within cost $c-24$.
--
--   Part 2 is the strengthened induction hypothesis needed for loops. The item-level costs (e.g. $+25$ for a conditional, $+30$ per loop test) are chosen to dominate the compiled code, so all cost bounds proved in the item language transfer verbatim to the stack machine and hence, via `multipliesAt_of_exec`, to the Turing machine.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Item states `IState S` have item stacks `G`, input pointer `ip` and output `out`; `encI s` writes each item stack $G_i$ as the concatenation of item encodings on stack $i$ and leaves the four extra scratch stacks ($s_q, s_1, s_2, s_3$ = indices $S,\dots,S+3$) empty. `Runs` asserts an exact final state and an upper bound on the cost; `IExec` carries the exact item-level cost.
-- source:
--   A. Schönhage, V. Strassen, Schnelle Multiplikation großer Zahlen, Computing 7 (1971), 281–292 (main theorem: O(n log n log log n) multitape TM multiplication). Lemma `NLogN.compile_correct` of the Lean development NLogN/IComp.lean (lines 159–206), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

-- Schönhage–Strassen multitape-TM development (NLogN), generated by skeleton subtraction.

import Definitions.Def_IntMul_SS_Defs2

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000

open NLogN
open IntMul
variable {S P : ℕ}
variable {I : List Sym} {prog : Fin P → Stmt (S + 4) P}

theorem NLogN.compile_correct {iprog : Fin P → IStmt S P} (hprog : ∀ j, prog j = compile (iprog j))
    {p : IStmt S P} {s s' : IState S} {c : ℕ} (h : IExec I iprog p s s' c) :
    Runs I prog (compile p) (encI s) (encI s') c ∧
      ∀ i f b, p = .loop i f b →
        Runs I prog (inner i f b) (encF i f s) ((encI s').push (s3 S) 0) (c - 24) ∧ 24 ≤ c := by sorry
